WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.060
Speaker 1: All right. So these right&nbsp;
wing stories about people crying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:07.500
they've become so ridiculous that they're&nbsp;
essentially parodies of themselves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:12.840
I'm not really going to show you one of&nbsp;
those here. From Republican Senator Ted Cruz,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:17.400
although it certainly reminded me of it. But&nbsp;
this actually is one of these stories about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:22.260
people coming up to me. The prototypical one that&nbsp;
Trump tells as, oh, you know, we're the people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:26.460
that arrested last week. He said the people that&nbsp;
arrested me were crying when they arrested me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:34.860
The the sort of subtext of this particular&nbsp;
story from Ted Cruz is that whenever he lands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:41.280
at different airports, police just flock to him,&nbsp;
police flock to him, and they just all support him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:47.640
so much. And this is again, it's dubious. It's&nbsp;
always dubious when Ted Cruz in this clip, he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:52.980
going to say it's a dozen dozen police officers&nbsp;
or whatever. It's probably happened at some point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:58.680
that a police officer came up to Ted Cruz and&nbsp;
gave him a patch or whatever Cruz says here. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:06.060
there's a very significant political utility to&nbsp;
these particular types of stories. So listen to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:09.840
this one and then we're going to talk about it.
Speaker 2: The most striking place to fly into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:18.180
Chicago island. It's Chicago O'Hare Airport.&nbsp;
I am with some regularity, met by ten, 12,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:26.100
15 uniformed police officers who come. They shake&nbsp;
my hand, they give me patches. I've got my office&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:34.129
patches of the Chicago P.D. that are displayed in&nbsp;
my office because those cops are so under assault.&nbsp;

00:01:34.129 --> 00:01:40.860
Speaker 1: But were they crying when they met&nbsp;
you, sir? Okay. So it's what is the point of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:48.060
these stories at this point in time? The point is&nbsp;
that it would be extremely dangerous and damaging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:56.940
to Republicans for it to become known that there&nbsp;
is plenty of military and law enforcement that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.940 --> 00:02:02.340
supports Democrats. And in fact, when you look&nbsp;
at the 2020 presidential election, one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:07.260
interesting things about, yes, the military leans&nbsp;
right. There is no question about that. But one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:14.580
of the interesting things about the military vote&nbsp;
in 2020 is that it was one of the highest levels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:19.260
of support for the Democratic candidate that&nbsp;
Joe Biden received from members of the military&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:24.300
than had been seen by Democrats for a very long&nbsp;
time. I don't remember the exact numbers. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:28.740
don't really matter. But the point is, compared to&nbsp;
past elections, it was a higher level of support.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:37.980
And this is all because Republicans want and to&nbsp;
some degree have obtained a monopoly on certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:44.220
elements of political discourse, law and order,&nbsp;
or, as Trump once called it, law and order,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:50.640
auto insurance, very, very important issue, but&nbsp;
something else altogether. Republicans use this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:55.080
law and order messaging. We are for law and order&nbsp;
when it comes to immigration, when it comes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:59.760
street crime, when it comes to demonstrations,&nbsp;
when it comes to all of these different things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:08.340
And they portray themselves as the arbiters of law&nbsp;
and order. And so they do that to appeal to voters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:15.780
who prioritize safety and who prioritize security.&nbsp;
Now understand that it's total B.S. It's complete&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:23.160
and total utter B.S. The reality is law and order&nbsp;
means we want our political enemies targeted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:27.660
but we don't want to be targeted when we do the&nbsp;
same things. Law and order means we support the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:35.340
police, except when it's the D.C. Capitol Police&nbsp;
trying to beat off rioters on January 6th of 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:41.460
or we law and order. Except when it's not law and&nbsp;
order, no one is above the law except when it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:46.740
Trump. And then why isn't he above the law? He&nbsp;
should be above the law. That's the messaging. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:51.420
if it became known that they aren't actually the&nbsp;
party of law and order, it would be very damaging.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:58.800
Nationalism and patriotism. Another element of&nbsp;
this supposedly. So remember, it's supposedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:10.440
pro-police narrative. The Republican Party often&nbsp;
translates or utilizes stated support for police&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:17.220
in the military as a point of national pride.&nbsp;
They must love the country more because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:20.640
support police and police support them. But that&nbsp;
doesn't really make any sense. Or the military,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:24.660
again, and particularly when there's&nbsp;
a crisis or a conflict or a war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:30.600
as we saw during the Iraq war under George W&nbsp;
Bush's presidency, that can actually be pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:36.060
useful. It taps into feelings of loyalty and&nbsp;
duty among many in the country. And then thirdly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:42.480
this narrative about how much police love Ted&nbsp;
Cruz. It's to draw a distinction with Democrats.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:48.960
You emphasize, you know, we have the support of&nbsp;
police and members of the military, and that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:56.460
because Democrats are weak or they're unpatriotic&nbsp;
or whatever. And if Democrats are seen as being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.460 --> 00:05:01.500
critical of law enforcement, then that contrast&nbsp;
is even even more. More useful. So it's another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:07.020
one of these. They come up to me with tears in&nbsp;
my eyes. Stories from Cancun. Ted Cruz. But it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:16.380
part of a much larger political area of political&nbsp;
strategy that if Republicans were to lose and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:20.040
should lose it because it's all completely&nbsp;
fabricated, would be very damaging to them.

